---
import { linkInstagramMentions } from "../../utils/instagramMentions"

const credits = linkInstagramMentions(
  "At @bartemp.bkk for @nonnonnon_bangkok<br />Mutant Club, 3rd Oct."
)
const thanks1 = linkInstagramMentions("Thank you so much @maehappyair @nonnonnon_bangkok")
const thanks2 = linkInstagramMentions(
  "<strong>Special thank</strong> @krung_khet for helping me setup the sound and test the website"
)
const thanks3 = linkInstagramMentions("<strong>Special thank</strong> @pin_natthamon for the clips")
---

<div class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
  <p class="mb-2 text-sm opacity-80" set:html={credits}></p>
  <p class="mb-6 text-sm opacity-80">Post-screen strategy for low-tech rooms</p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    This piece grows directly from a practical question: what if the venue has no projector, and
    sometimes almost no lighting? If I cannot assume projector or lighting, I ask the audience to
    become the light system.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    I invite people to scan a website, grant camera and flashlight permission, and turn their phones
    into moving strobes. Their batteries become my spotlight; their recording devices become part of
    the stage instead of only documenting it. Later in the set, I may ask them to scan again if the
    crowd is smaller than expected.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    I am interested in DIY methods that turn audience assets (phones, attention, batteries) and space
    assets (walls, darkness, existing light) into part of the piece itself. The goal is not only to
    make it work, but to make the room feel visually strong. At the same time, I try to rely less on
    dragging in a big screen as the default solution — while still craving the scale of massive LED
    architecture, the kind you can feel from far away in large US venues.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <img
      src="/images/las-vegas-sphere.jpg"
      alt="Las Vegas Sphere, a large LED dome venue in the US"
      class="w-full"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm opacity-70">
      Las Vegas Sphere. Photo: Matt Walter, CC BY 2.0 via Wikimedia Commons
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 class="font-light text-xl mb-3 mt-8">How the system works</h2>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Each phone joins through the browser. The site requests camera and flashlight access, then drives
    flashlight pulses from each device camera stream. Strobes do not sync together — each phone runs
    its own tempo, randomly set between 120 and 180 BPM, so the crowd becomes a distributed,
    out-of-phase light field.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4">
    Before and late in my set, I would beg the audiences to give me some more light as an inspiration
    — as a spotlight — and in exchange, I would continue to give them my sound back.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <a
      href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DP4E_7Mkz0-/?img_index=1"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      class="hover:underline"
    >
      Gimme the light performance (Instagram)
    </a>
  </figure>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <img src="/images/qr.png" alt="QR code to join Gimme the light" class="w-48" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm opacity-70">Scan to join</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-neutral-800">
    <p class="text-sm mb-2" set:html={thanks1}></p>
    <p class="text-sm mb-2">Thank you all audiences (and folks who help gimme some lights)</p>
    <p class="text-sm mb-2" set:html={thanks2}></p>
    <p class="text-sm" set:html={thanks3}></p>
  </div>
</div>
